WITS HUMAN RESEARCH ETHICS COMMITTEE (MEDICAL) 

AMENDMENT NOTIFICATION 

HE²AT CENTRE DATA MANAGEMENT PLAN VERSION 2.2 

Document Title: NIH HE²AT Centre Data Management Plan - Infrastructure Transition Amendment 

Version: 2.2 (Revised) 

Previous Version: 2.1 (September 2024) 

Amendment Date: August 2025 

HREC Reference Numbers: RP1 (220605), RP2 (220606) 

Principal Investigators: Matthew Chersich (Wits PHR) 

Contact: Craig Parker (Craig.parker@witsphr.org) 

NATURE OF AMENDMENT: Temporary infrastructure transition during Year 5 to enhance data management capabilities through cloud infrastructure migration. This amendment documents a transition period where dual infrastructure capability (UCT on-premise and WHC-managed cloud) is maintained, with complete migration to cloud by end of Year 5. All existing ethical safeguards are maintained and enhanced. 

PARTICIPANT IMPACT: No direct impact on participants. Enhanced data protection and security measures only. 

RISK ASSESSMENT: Risk reduced through enhanced security, 24/7 monitoring, and automated backup capabilities. 

SUMMARY OF CHANGES 

DMP Section 

Specific Changes Made 

Rationale and Ethical Considerations 

Section 3.4 
Overview of Data Management Workflow 

Added new subsection clarifying Year 5 as a transition period with temporary dual infrastructure capability, ending with complete cloud migration 

Provides transparent timeline for infrastructure changes. Ensures stakeholder understanding that this is a temporary transition, not permanent hybrid model. No impact on participant data protection. 

Section 4.1.2 
Original Study Data 

Added "during the transition period to WHC-managed cloud infrastructure" and specified dual capability as temporary measure 

Clarifies temporary nature of dual infrastructure. Maintains all existing data protections while enhancing security during transition. 

Section 4.2 
Climate/Weather Data 

Added "during the transition period from WHC-managed cloud services" with emphasis on enhanced auto-scaling and accessibility 

Improves research efficiency and data accessibility during transition whilst maintaining all access controls and authentication requirements. 

Section 5.1 
Data Transfer Agreement 

Simplified contractual language, removed detailed DTA requirements, focused on compliance and data management procedures 

Streamlines document for ethics review while maintaining all essential compliance requirements. Reduces complexity without compromising participant protection. 

Section 6.1 
Pre-processing 

Added "during the transition period to WHC-managed cloud infrastructure with enhanced security and automated backup systems" 

Strengthens data integrity during processing with automated systems. Provides additional safeguards during transition period. 

Section 6.4 
Database Population 

Added reference to cloud-native database services during transition with automated backup and enhanced disaster recovery 

Improves data preservation and recovery capabilities, reducing risk of data loss during critical transition period. 

Section 8 
Data Analysis Platform 

Added "cloud-based computational resources with GPU acceleration" available during transition period 

Enhances analytical capabilities for researchers during transition while maintaining all existing access controls and security protocols. 

Section 10.4 
Data Sharing Procedures 

NEW CRITICAL CHANGE: "All projects, regardless of whether they involve internal HE²AT Center Consortium members or external researchers, must go through the DAC for data access approval" 

Ensures equitable data sharing and comprehensive tracking of all projects. Strengthens governance by applying consistent standards to all data access requests. Enhances transparency and accountability. 

Section 12.3 
Data Access Committee 

Updated to emphasize DAC evaluates ALL data access requests (consortium and external) with consistent ethical and scientific standards 

Removes any perception of preferential access. Ensures uniform application of ethical standards. Strengthens governance framework. 

Section 14.1 
Data Transfer, Storage and Encryption 

Added "during the transition period to WHC-managed cloud infrastructure" and specified "AES-256 encryption standard for both UCT servers and cloud storage" 

Upgrades encryption standards across all infrastructure. Provides superior data protection during transition with industry-standard encryption. 

Section 14.2 
Network Security 

Added "during the transition period from WHC-managed cloud security architecture with 24/7 Security Operations Centre (SOC) monitoring and advanced threat detection systems" 

Significantly enhances security oversight with professional 24/7 monitoring. Provides superior threat detection compared to standard academic infrastructure. 

Section 14.3 
Local Authentication and Authorisation 

Added "alongside cloud-specific access controls and automated compliance reporting for streamlined governance oversight during the transition period" 

Enhances access control and provides automated compliance monitoring. Improves audit capabilities and regulatory oversight. 

Section 15 
Roles and Responsibilities 

Removed individual names and contact details from main table, generalized to institutional roles with key functions 

Facilitates document maintenance without requiring amendments for personnel changes. Contact information maintained separately for easy updates. 

Section 17 
Cloud Migration 

REMOVED from main document and restructured as Annex 1: Transition Implementation Details 

Streamlines main document for ethics review while preserving detailed implementation information in annex format. 

NEW Annex 1 
Transition Implementation Details 

NEW SECTION: Comprehensive 4-phase transition strategy with timeline, data provider communication framework, and generic email template 

Provides transparent implementation roadmap. Ensures proper stakeholder communication. Documents systematic approach to minimize disruption. 

All other sections 

Document structure simplified from 17 to 16 sections. Removed complex contractual language throughout. Maintained all ethical frameworks and participant protections. 

Improves readability for ethics review. Focuses on data management procedures rather than legal complexity. No reduction in participant protections. 

ETHICAL IMPACT ASSESSMENT 

Participant Rights: All existing participant rights maintained and enhanced through improved security infrastructure during transition period. 

Data Protection: Significant enhancement through: 

• AES-256 encryption across all infrastructure 
• 24/7 professional security monitoring during transition 
• Automated backup and disaster recovery systems 
• Advanced threat detection capabilities 
• Dual infrastructure redundancy during transition 

Consent and Transparency: Clear communication framework for data providers about transition. Emphasis on temporary nature of changes with defined endpoint. 

Governance Enhancement: Universal DAC review requirement strengthens oversight. Consistent application of ethical standards to all data access requests. 

Risk Mitigation: Transition period approach minimizes operational risks through: 
• Phased implementation over 12 months 
• Dual system operation for continuity 
• Enhanced security throughout transition 
• Professional monitoring and support 

CONFIRMATION 

These amendments document a temporary infrastructure transition during Year 5 that enhances data protection and research capabilities. The transition will be complete by end of Year 5 with full migration to secure cloud infrastructure. All existing ethical safeguards are maintained and enhanced. No changes to research methodology, participant recruitment, or data collection procedures. 

The universal DAC review requirement ensures equitable and transparent data governance for all users. 

All changes maintain full POPIA compliance and enhance alignment with international data protection standards.